  "transcript": "Medicare Advantage, Part C, lets Dennis receive his Medicare benefits through a private plan that Medicare pays a fixed monthly amount per member. To join he must have Parts A and B and live in the plan’s service area. The plan must cover everything Original Medicare covers, except hospice, which Original Medicare continues to pay, and most plans include Part D drug coverage and extras such as dental, vision, hearing and fitness benefits. Dennis keeps paying his Part B premium, and the plan may charge its own premium, often zero. The plan’s rules, networks and cost sharing replace Medicare’s.\n\nAdvantage plans come in the designs you know. HMOs require a primary care physician and referrals and cover nothing out of network except emergencies. PPOs pay more in network and less out. Private fee-for-service plans let members see any provider who accepts the plan’s terms. Special needs plans serve people with specific conditions, those in institutions or those also on Medicaid. Unlike Original Medicare, every Advantage plan has an annual out-of-pocket maximum for Part A and B services, a real protection. Plans may not deny enrollment for health conditions; ESRD patients may also enroll. Medicare publishes star ratings for quality.\n\nDennis may join an Advantage plan during his initial Medicare enrollment period. Each year the annual election period runs October 15 through December 7, when anyone may join, switch or drop an Advantage or Part D plan for January 1. A Medicare Advantage open enrollment period from January 1 through March 31 lets a current Advantage member switch once to another Advantage plan or return to Original Medicare with a Part D plan. Special enrollment periods follow moves, loss of other coverage, Medicaid changes and plan terminations. A member cannot hold a Medicare supplement policy while in an Advantage plan.\n\nPart D is optional outpatient drug coverage sold by private insurers, either as a stand-alone prescription drug plan paired with Original Medicare or built into an Advantage plan. Each plan has a formulary, a list of covered drugs arranged in tiers with different cost sharing, and may use prior authorization and step therapy. Enrollment windows match Part B’s initial period and the annual election period. The standard benefit has a deductible, 615 dollars in 2026, then cost sharing of about 25 percent until the member’s out-of-pocket spending reaches the annual cap, 2,100 dollars in 2026, after which covered drugs cost nothing for the rest of the year.\n\nDennis should enroll in Part D when first eligible or keep creditable drug coverage, such as a retiree plan at least as good as Part D. For each month he goes without creditable coverage after his initial period, he owes a permanent penalty of one percent of the national base premium added to his Part D premium for life. Extra Help, the low-income subsidy, pays most Part D costs for people with limited income and resources. A Medicare Prescription Payment Plan lets members spread out-of-pocket drug costs across the year in monthly installments. Employers must tell retirees each year whether their drug coverage is creditable.\n\nDennis has two coherent choices. Road one: Original Medicare, a Medicare supplement policy to fill the deductibles and coinsurance, and a stand-alone Part D plan. He may see any provider who takes Medicare, nationwide, with predictable costs and higher monthly premiums. Road two: a Medicare Advantage plan, usually with drug coverage and extras, lower premiums, a network and referral rules, and an out-of-pocket maximum. He may switch between roads during election periods, but returning to a supplement later may require medical underwriting outside Maryland’s protected windows. Lesson 44 explains the supplement side; Lesson 45 explains Maryland’s protections."
